## Glossary

* [Ad exchange](/docs/glossary/ad-exchange.md): \*Advertising\*. A digital marketplace where advertisers and publishers can buy and sell display, video, and mobile advertising.
* [Ad impression](/docs/glossary/ad-impression.md): \*Advertising\*. One occurence of an advertisement displaying on a screen.
* [Ad unit](/docs/glossary/ad-unit.md): \*Advertising\*. The container that holds the advertising copy and graphics for a digital advertisement on a screen.
* [Affiliate marketing](/docs/glossary/affiliate-marketing.md): \*Marketing\*. An activity in which companies offer to pay individuals or other businesses to promote their products or services.
* [Affirmative consent](/docs/glossary/affirmative-consent.md): \*Reputation\*. The informed, explicit acceptance that a recipient granted you to receive your email.
* [Allowlist](/docs/glossary/allow-list.md): \*Internet Security\*. A curated selection of domains, email addresses, or IP addresses that a service or system has granted explicit access.
* [Automated email](/docs/glossary/automated-email.md): \*Marketing\*. A system that allows the sending of messages with content relevant to individual recipients.
* [Autoresponder](/docs/glossary/autoresponder.md): \*Marketing\*. A system that generates email responses to actions and events.
* [Bayesian filter](/docs/glossary/bayesian-filter.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A statistical technique that detects email spam.
* [Blocklist](/docs/glossary/blocklist.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A list of IP addresses, email addresses, or domains known to ISPs or list providers for sending unsolicited or unwanted emails.
* [Blocked email messages](/docs/glossary/blocks.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A temporary rejection of messages sent from your IP address.
* [Bounced email messages](/docs/glossary/bounces.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A permanent rejection of messages sent from your IP address.
* [Bulk email service](/docs/glossary/bulk-email-service.md): \*Marketing\*. Software or service through which one sender can send one or many email messages to a large list of recipients.
* [What is Call Whisper?](/docs/glossary/call-whisper.md): Call Whisper, also commonly referred to as Call Screening, involves playing a message to the callee while the caller continues to hear ringing. It can provide additional information such as the source or purpose of the call to the callee before the call begins and even allow the callee to accept or reject the call based on that information.
* [Campaigns](/docs/glossary/campaigns.md): \*Marketing\*. The act of sending customized email messages to a group of targeted recipient segments.
* [CAN-SPAM Act of 2003](/docs/glossary/can-spam.md): \*Regulation\*. US law that lists the guidelines, requirements, and penalties regarding the sending of commercial bulk email.
* [Canonicalization](/docs/glossary/canonicalization.md): \*Internet standard\*. The standardization of email messages formatting that enables consistent hashed values for validating email messages.
* [Canadian Anti-Spam Law of 2014](/docs/glossary/casl.md): \*Regulation\*. Canadian law that lists the guidelines, requirements, and penalties regarding the sending of commercial bulk email.
* [Categories](/docs/glossary/categories.md): \*Analytics\*. One or more topical tags applied to an email message that aids in analyzing email campaign effectiveness.&#x20;
* [Content delivery network](/docs/glossary/cdn.md): \*Service Provider\*. A global network of servers that stores and serves copies of website content to website visitors using the server geographically closest to each website visitor.&#x20;
* [Challenge-response spam filtering](/docs/glossary/challenge-response.md): \*Internet Security\*. A type of spam filter that replies with a challenge to the given sender.
* [Clicks and unique clicks](/docs/glossary/clicks.md): \*Analytics\*. A record of each time a customer clicks any link in your Twilio Email email messages.
* [Canonical Name record (CNAME)](/docs/glossary/cname.md): \*Internet standard\*. A DNS record that maps one hostname in a domain to another hostname instead of an IP address.
* [Complaint](/docs/glossary/complaint.md): \*Reputation\*. A record of an email recipient reporting an email message as spam.
* [CPS](/docs/glossary/cps.md): CPS (calls per second) is the rate at which Twilio executes outbound calls for an account.&#x20;
* [Click-through rate](/docs/glossary/ctr.md): \*Analytics\*. A metric of interest in content linked in an email message expressed as a percentage of recipients that click a link in your email message.
* [Custom fields](/docs/glossary/custom-fields.md): \*Configuration\*. Original contact attribute added to your email contacts.
* [Deferral](/docs/glossary/deferrals.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A deferred status can occur when an ISP or mailbox provider can't accept email from your IP address.
* [Deliverability](/docs/glossary/deliverability.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. The measure of how many emails sent that reach their intended recipients without issue.
* [Delivery](/docs/glossary/deliveries.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A confirmation that an end recipient received the email that the sender had sent.
* [Demand-side platform (DSP)](/docs/glossary/demand-side-platform.md): \*Advertising\*. Software that helps advertisers or ad agencies buy digital ad inventory across publishers, supply-side platforms, and ad exchanges.
* [Direct response advertising](/docs/glossary/direct-response-advertising.md): \*Advertising\*. An advertising tactic where ad content encourages or prompts viewers of an ad to act or to engage with the ad directly.
* [DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM)](/docs/glossary/dkim.md): \*Internet standard\*. A domain-based email authentication protocol that helps ISPs better identify legitimate email senders.
* [Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance (DMARC)](/docs/glossary/dmarc.md): \*Internet Security\*, \*Internet Standard\*. A domain-based policy for determining the authenticity of the person or service sending email on behalf of a domain.
* [Domain Name System](/docs/glossary/dns.md): \*Internet standard\*. A hierarchical system of software and systems that translate a fully qualified domain name into an IP address.
* [Domain authentication](/docs/glossary/domain-authentication.md): \*Internet Security\*. Protocols and policies that specify IP addresses and domains as proof of identity of an individual or organization sending email messages.
* [Domain](/docs/glossary/domain.md): \*Internet standard\*. A human-readable branding for an individual or organization's presence on the internet.
* [Drip campaign](/docs/glossary/drip-campaign.md): \*Marketing\*. An email marketing strategy that sends engagement email messages on a schedule.
* [Drops](/docs/glossary/drops.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. The removal of an email message to a specific recipient to protect the sender's reputation.
* [Email API integration](/docs/glossary/email-api-integration.md): \*API\*. The facilitation of automating key functions through the synchronization of a sender's email service provider with their platform and software tools.
* [Email Authentication](/docs/glossary/email-authentication.md): \*Internet Security\*. Technical standards to help ISPs and other receivers validate the identity of an email sender.
* [Email harvesting](/docs/glossary/email-harvesting.md): \*Reputation\*. The process of obtaining lists of valid email addresses other than affirmative consent of the recipients for the purpose of sending bulk email.
* [Email marketing](/docs/glossary/email-marketing.md): \*Marketing\*. The promotion of a commercial product or service conveyed in an email message.
* [Email reputation score](/docs/glossary/email-reputation-score.md): \*Reputation\*. The rating an internet service provider or inbox provider sets for email sent from your email server IP addresses.
* [Email service provider (ESP)](/docs/glossary/email-service-provider.md): \*Service Provider\*. A service organization that provides transactional and marketing email services.
* [Expandable Banner](/docs/glossary/expandable-banner.md): \*Advertising\*. A design of a digital advertisement that increases in size when a viewer clicks or hovers over the ad.
* [Expired](/docs/glossary/expired.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. The removal of recipient email address from a contact list when an SMTP server couldn't deliver an email message to the recipient within 72 hours.
* [Feedback loop](/docs/glossary/feedback-loop.md): \*Reputation\*. A process where an service provider forwards emails reported as spam to a sender for removal.
* [Flighting](/docs/glossary/flighting-in-advertising.md): \*Advertising\*. The time in which an advertising campaign runs.
* [Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN)](/docs/glossary/fqdn.md): \*Internet standard\*. A human-readable label that identifies a specific server on the internet.
* [Frequency capping](/docs/glossary/frequency-capping.md): \*Advertising\*. The setting of a maximum number of times a specific viewer sees a specific ad.&#x20;
* [General Data Protection Regulation of 2018](/docs/glossary/gdpr.md): \*Regulation\*. European Union law that regulates the handling of personal data and outlines the rights individuals have regarding their data.
* [Header](/docs/glossary/header.md): \*Internet standard\*. Metadata that informs email recipients and servers of the routing, formatting, and security of an email message.
* [Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP)](/docs/glossary/imap.md): \*Internet standard\*. The communication rules that systems follow to let you read and retrieve email messages from your inbox provider.
* [Glossary](/docs/glossary.md): A repository of the technical terms and jargon intrepid Twilio developers may encounter on their building journey.
* [Invalid Email](/docs/glossary/invalid-email.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. The removal of recipient email address that doesn't conform to the \[Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF)]\(https://www.ietf.org/) email address syntax standards from a contact list.
* [IP Address](/docs/glossary/ip-address.md): \*Internet standard\*. A unique numerical address that identifies a virtual location on the internet.
* [IP address warmup](/docs/glossary/ip-warmup.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. The establishment of a reputation as a legitimate email sender through the gradual and regular increase in the volume of email sent from one IP address.
* [Link branding](/docs/glossary/link-branding.md): \*Marketing\*. Change the domain for open and click tracking links to your domain.
* [Lists](/docs/glossary/lists.md): \*Marketing\*. Static collections of contacts to whom email messages can be sent.
* [Mail merge](/docs/glossary/mail-merge.md): \*Marketing\*. A process that generates customized email messages from a generic template and store of contact data.
* [Mailbox provider](/docs/glossary/mailbox-provider.md): \*Marketing\*. A service organization that provides transactional email services, marketing email services, or both.
* [Twilio Email message ID](/docs/glossary/message-id.md): \*Marketing\*. A unique identifier that Twilio Email generates and assigns to an email message for event identification purposes.
* [Mail Transfer Agent (MTA)](/docs/glossary/mta.md): \*Internet standard\*. A system that transfers email messages from one computer to another using a client-server application architecture.
* [Mail User Agent (MUA)](/docs/glossary/mua.md): \*Internet standard\*. An app that you use to compose and send email messages.
* [Mail Exchanger record (MX)](/docs/glossary/mx-record.md): \*Internet standard\*. A DNS record that maps the hostname in a domain that accepts email for the domain.
* [Native advertising](/docs/glossary/native-advertising.md): \*Advertising\*. A type of advertising that resembles the content of the surrounding medium.
* [What are North American Area Codes?](/docs/glossary/north-american-area-codes.md): North American Area Codes prefix phone numbers to route calls under the North American Numbering Plan (NANP), covering the US, Canada, US territories, and some Caribbean countries.
* [Open Rate](/docs/glossary/open-rate.md): \*Analytics\*. The frequency by which your customers open emails that you send.
* [Open relay](/docs/glossary/openrelay.md): \*Internet Security\*. An unsecured SMTP relay that routes email between domains.
* [Open & Unique Open](/docs/glossary/opens.md): \*Analytics\*. The record of each time a customer opens your Twilio Email email messages.
* [Peer-initiated invitations campaign](/docs/glossary/peer-invitations.md): \*Marketing\*. A email marketing campaign designed to get customers to invite their friends and family.
* [Phishing](/docs/glossary/phishing.md): \*Internet Security\*. The act of masquerading as a trusted business to acquire personally identifiable information.
* [Preheader](/docs/glossary/preheader.md): \*Marketing\*. The short summary text that follows the subject line when you view an email in your inbox.
* [Programmatic media buying](/docs/glossary/programmatic-media-buying.md): \*Advertising\*. Process of buying paid media through sophisticated software that automates how the buying, placing, and optimizing of paid media.
* [Pointer record (PTR)](/docs/glossary/ptr.md): \*Internet standard\*. A DNS record that maps an IP address to one hostname in a domain.
* [Rate limiting](/docs/glossary/rate-limiting.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. See Throttling.
* [Reconfirmation email](/docs/glossary/reconfirmation.md): \*Reputation\*. An email message sent to existing recipients asking them to confirm that they still want your messages.
* [Request](/docs/glossary/request.md): \*Analytics\*. The record of an attempt to send an email through Twilio Email.
* [Reseller email account](/docs/glossary/reseller-email-account.md): \*Service Provider\*. Email account created after a company entered into a partnership with Twilio.
* [Reserved fields](/docs/glossary/reserved-fields.md): \*Configuration\*. Predefined and protected attributes provided for email contacts.
* [Reverse DNS](/docs/glossary/reverse-dns.md): \*Internet Standard\*. Method for systems to resolve an IP address from a domain name.
* [Scheduled Emails](/docs/glossary/scheduled-emails.md): \*Marketing\*. Configuring a system to send email messages starting at a given date and time.
* [Segmentation](/docs/glossary/segmentation.md): \*Marketing\*. The practice of channeling email traffic based on the intent and audience of email messages.
* [Segments](/docs/glossary/segments.md): \*Marketing\*. A dynamic collection of contacts grouped based on criteria you define.
* [Sender authentication](/docs/glossary/sender-authentication.md): \*Internet Security\*. The process that shows ISPs you gave your mailbox provider permission to send emails on your behalf.
* [Sender ID](/docs/glossary/sender-id.md): \*Internet Security\*. A deprecated email authentication standard that checked that a domain authorized a given email address to send on its behalf.
* [Senders](/docs/glossary/senders.md): Identity of the individual, position, or organization sending an email message.
* [Sends](/docs/glossary/sends.md): \*Analytics\*. A request for Twilio Email to deliver email to your recipients.
* [Single send](/docs/glossary/single-send.md): \*Marketing\*. A non-recurring email message that communicates non-automated, non-sequential content.
* [SIP INVITEs](/docs/glossary/sip-invites.md): A SIP INVITE is a SIP request message that initiates a SIP call. 
   
  A SIP INVITE is made up of lines of text. The first line in an INVITE is called a Request-Line, which is followed by more lines of text called headers. The headers contain information about the INVITE, such as the identity of the caller, whether the INVITE was forwarded before being sent to the recipient, and the number of times a call may be forwarded. 
* [SMTP Provider](/docs/glossary/smtp-provider.md): \*Service Provider\*. A vendor that helps send large quantities of email messages to recipients.
* [SMTP relay](/docs/glossary/smtp-relay.md): \*Internet standard\*. A system that sends email between SMTP servers on different domains.
* [SMTP Server](/docs/glossary/smtp-server.md): \*Internet standard\*. An app or server that sends email and reacts to response codes from receiving servers.
* [SMTP service](/docs/glossary/smtp-service.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. Third-party managed service that delivers email messages to a large audience.
* [Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP)](/docs/glossary/smtp.md): \*Internet standard\*. The communication rules that systems follow for the reliable and efficient transfer of email messages.
* [Spam Filter](/docs/glossary/spam-filter.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. Algorithms that identify and remediate unwanted email messages.
* [Spam folder](/docs/glossary/spam-folder.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A location in a recipient's inbox into which the inbox provider routes email of questionable use or provenance.
* [Spam report](/docs/glossary/spam-reports.md): \*Reputation\*. A feedback mechanism that identifies electronic messages as abusing the transmission medium and reports them to an authority for remediation.
* [Spam Traps](/docs/glossary/spam-traps.md): \*Reputation\*. Email addresses created without active owners to identify spammers and senders with poor data quality practices.
* [Spam](/docs/glossary/spam.md): \*Reputation\*. An email message sent to a recipient that they didn't request or want.
* [Sender Policy Framework (SPF)](/docs/glossary/spf.md): \*Internet Security\*, \*Internet Standard\*. An email authentication standard that sets which servers can send email from their domain.
* [Spoofing](/docs/glossary/spoofing.md): \*Internet Security\*. A technique where a falsified sender email addresses attempts to trick recipients into opening an email message.
* [Subscriber list management](/docs/glossary/subscriber-list-management.md): \*Marketing\*. The validation that subscribers found in your contact lists remain active and engaged with your email marketing.
* [Suspicious Sender](/docs/glossary/suspicious-sender.md): \*Reputation\*. An email sender who sends email messages to recipients that never granted explicit permission to that sender.
* [Reserved substitution tags](/docs/glossary/system-fields.md): \*Configuration\*. Placeholder labels that get replaced with personalized content in an email campaign.
* [Throttling](/docs/glossary/throttling.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A recipient inbox provider reduces throughput of email messages below the level at which sender mail server sent the email messages.
* [Time zone](/docs/glossary/timezone.md): \*Internet standard\*. Local time, based on your current geographic location on the earth.
* [Transport Layer Security (TLS)](/docs/glossary/tls.md): \*Internet Security\*, \*Internet Standard\*. The set of rules that encrypts internet traffic between systems.
* [What is a Time-based One-time Password (TOTP)?](/docs/glossary/totp.md): A standardized algorithm that uses the current time as an input and generates unique numeric passwords.
* [Transactional email](/docs/glossary/transactional-email.md): \*Marketing\*. An email message sent in response to a user interaction with a web application.
* [Triggered action](/docs/glossary/triggered-actions.md): \*Marketing\*. A task executed once a customer meets a pre-defined condition.
* [Triggered email](/docs/glossary/triggered-email.md): \*Marketing\*. An email message sent in response to specific events or actions.
* [Trusted sender](/docs/glossary/trusted-sender.md): \*Reputation\*. An email sender who only sends email messages to those recipients that granted explicit permission to that sender.
* [Two Factor Authentication (2FA)](/docs/glossary/two-factor-authentication.md): \*Internet Security\*. Use of two or more means of user identification to grant access to a system.
* [Undelivered Email](/docs/glossary/undelivered-email.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A category of reasons why an email message wasn't delivered to a recipient's inbox.
* [Unknown User](/docs/glossary/unknown-user.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. The removal of recipient email address that doesn't exist on the recipient email server from a contact list.
* [Unsubscribe](/docs/glossary/unsubscribes.md): \*Email Deliverability\*. A recipient's voluntary decision to remove themself from a sender's bulk email service.
* [Urchin Tracking Module (UTM) parameters](/docs/glossary/utm-parameters.md): \*Analytics\*. Five URL parameters added to links that marketers use to track online marketing campaigns.
* [Web API](/docs/glossary/web-api.md): A web API is a programming interface that allows web servers and web browsers to connect and enable account and data collection for services like email.
* [Application-to-Person Messaging](/docs/glossary/what-a2p-sms-application-person-messaging.md): Application-to-Person messaging (A2P) is any kind of message traffic in which a person is receiving messages from an application rather than another individual, and which is not expected to receive a reply. A2P message includes, but is not limited to, marketing communications, appointment reminders, chatbots, notifications, and one-time passwords (OTPs) or PIN codes.
* [Alphanumeric Sender ID](/docs/glossary/what-alphanumeric-sender-id.md): Your company name or brand displayed as the sender ID for one-way text messaging.
* [What are Masked Phone Numbers?](/docs/glossary/what-are-masked-phone-numbers.md): Masked Phone Numbers are a common pattern to anonymize communication between multiple parties and hide participant phone numbers. Instead of dialing directly from phone to phone, users communicate via a third ('proxy') phone number that forwards a call to the eventual destination. 
* [What are SMS Notifications?](/docs/glossary/what-are-sms-notifications.md): SMS Notifications are out-of-band text messages sent in response to events or transactions which occur somewhere else. While often used as a marketing tool to increase the percentage of returning visitors, SMS notifications are very useful for organization and public safety purposes as well.
* [What are WebSockets?](/docs/glossary/what-are-websockets.md): A WebSocket is a persistent bi-directional communication channel between a client (e.g. a browser) and a backend service. In contrast with HTTP request/response connections, websockets can transport any number of protocols and provide server-to-client message delivery without polling.
* [What is Direct Inward Dialing (DID)?](/docs/glossary/what-direct-inward-dialing-did.md): Direct Inward Dialing (DID) is a telephone service that allows a phone number to ring through directly to a specific phone at a business instead of going to a menu or a queue and needing to dial an extension. A phone number that is used like this is often called a "DID" (and multiple numbers are called "DIDs").
* [What is E.164?](/docs/glossary/what-e164.md): A numbering plan standard that provides a globally unique number for each device on the PSTN. These devices can then route phone calls and text messages to individual devices in different countries.
* [What is a Call Center?](/docs/glossary/what-is-a-call-center.md): A call center is the central point where businesses handle inbound and outbound customer phone calls. Learn how call centers differ from contact centers and the software that powers them.
* [What's an SMS Short Code?](/docs/glossary/what-is-a-short-code.md): A numeric sender ID used for text messaging containing fewer digits than typical phone numbers.
* [What is a String Identifier (SID)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-a-sid.md): A unique key string that Twilio uses to identify specific resources.
* [What is an SMS Gateway?](/docs/glossary/what-is-a-sms-gateway.md): An SMS Gateway enables a computer to send and receive SMS text messages to and from an SMS capable device over the global telecommunications network (normally to a mobile phone). The SMS Gateway translates the message sent, and makes it compatible for delivery over the network to be able to reach the recipient.
* [What is a Webhook?](/docs/glossary/what-is-a-webhook.md): An event-driven communication that sends data between apps using custom HTTP POST request to a URL.
* [Ahoy!](/docs/glossary/what-is-ahoy.md): Ahoy, a signal word originally used to call a ship, was once a standard way to greet others and was Alexander Graham Bell's suggested greeting for answering the telephone.
* [API Key](/docs/glossary/what-is-an-api-key.md): An Application Programming Interface (API) Key is a unique identifier that is used to authenticate a developer or program to an API.
* [API](/docs/glossary/what-is-an-api.md): An Application Programming Interface (API) is provided by a service owner so that others may use the features and functions enabled by the service. APIs describe how a consumer will make requests of the service, and what they will receive in return.
* [What is an SMS?](/docs/glossary/what-is-an-sms-short-message-service.md): The text messaging service that telephone, internet, and mobile devices use.
* [Artificial Intelligence](/docs/glossary/what-is-artificial-intelligence.md): Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the ability of a computer to mimic human cognitive skills, such as learning and understanding.
* [Basic Authentication](/docs/glossary/what-is-basic-authentication.md): Basic Authentication is a method for an HTTP user agent (e.g., a web browser) to provide a username and password when making a request.
* [What is Call Routing?](/docs/glossary/what-is-call-routing.md): Call routing refers to the procedure of sending voice calls to a specific queue based on predetermined criteria. A call routing system is also known as an automatic call distributor (ACD).
* [What is Call Transcription?](/docs/glossary/what-is-call-transcription.md): Call transcription is the conversion of a voice or video call audio track into written words to be stored as plain text in a conversational language. Call transcription can either be live - as a call or event happens - or based on the recording of a past conversation.
* [What is Click-to-Call?](/docs/glossary/what-is-click-to-call.md): Click-to-call, sometimes called click-to-talk or click-to-dial, is a way to let people connect with a company representative by phone while they're browsing a website or in an app.
* [What are DTMF Tones?](/docs/glossary/what-is-dtmf.md): DTMF, or Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency tones, are in-band telecommunications signals sent over voice frequencies.  Commonly used over telephone lines, DTMF tones are also commonly called Touch Tones.
* [What is GSM-7 character encoding?](/docs/glossary/what-is-gsm-7-character-encoding.md): The original assignment of 128 alphanumeric characters accepted and displayed in text messages to numerical values for transmission across mobile networks.
* [Interactive Voice Response (IVR)](/docs/glossary/what-is-ivr.md): Interactive voice response (IVR), also known as a phone tree, provides an automated telephony system for callers using voice and touch-tones (DTMF).
* [What is Latency?](/docs/glossary/what-is-latency.md): Latency is the time delay between an event and its perception by an observer. In networking and telecommunications, latency is often used interchangeably with lag.
* [What is Live Chat?](/docs/glossary/what-is-live-chat.md): Live chat is a type of online chat distinguished by its simplicity and user accessibility. Live chat appears in a web browser or in mobile applications, usually via a small pop-up modal through which the visitor can exchange text messages with a chat operator in real time.
* [Masked Calling](/docs/glossary/what-is-masked-calling.md): Masked Calling is an ecommerce technique that protects buyers' and sellers' personal phone numbers by giving each party a temporary number to communicate for a set time.
* [What is a Mean Opinion Score (MOS)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-mean-opinion-score-mos.md): A Mean Opinion Score (MOS) is a numerical measure of human-judged overall quality. In telecommunications, MOS ranks the quality of voice and video sessions on a 1 to 5 scale.
* [What is an MMS?](/docs/glossary/what-is-mms.md): MMS, short for Multimedia Messaging Service, is a standard way to send multimedia such as pictures, videos, and other attachments over text messaging channels.
* [What is a Private Branch Exchange (PBX)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-pbx-private-branch-exchange.md): PBX, short for Private Branch Exchange, is a phone system in an enterprise that manages incoming and outgoing phone calls as well as an organization's internal communications.
* [What is Personally Identifiable Information (PII)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-personally-identifiable-information-pii.md): Any data of a degree of specificity that it can locate, identify, or contact a single person. This data includes phone numbers, national ID numbers, and email addresses. It covers any data that can be used, either on its own or with combined with other data, to contact, identify, or locate a person.
* [What is a Push Notification?](/docs/glossary/what-is-push-notification.md): When an app server delivers an alert separate from an end user request, that alert is called a push notification. Also known as a server push notification or push, these alerts target mobile devices but can also target web apps.
* [What is Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-session-initiation-protocol-sip.md): Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is a signalling protocol for initiating, terminating, and modifying user sessions over an IP network.  Most commonly, SIP is used for Voice Over IP (VoIP) services, but is also often used for other communications sessions such as video calls and instant messaging sessions.
* [What is SIP Trunking?](/docs/glossary/what-is-sip-trunking.md): SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) trunking routes phone calls over the internet instead of traditional phone lines, letting you provision global voice connectivity for your VoIP infrastructure at lower cost.
* [What is an SMS API?](/docs/glossary/what-is-sms-api-short-messaging-service.md): An SMS API is a software interface that lets code send and receive text messages through an SMS gateway, bridging the gap between telecommunications carrier networks and web applications built on standard web frameworks.
* [What is SMS Delivery or Deliverability?](/docs/glossary/what-is-sms-delivery-deliverability.md): SMS Delivery is a measure of the percentage of outgoing SMS and MMS messages which are received at their intended destination.  While sometimes referring to the status of a single message, SMS delivery usually is a rate of delivered versus intended messages and summarized as an 'SMS Delivery Rate.'
* [What is SMS Pumping Fraud?](/docs/glossary/what-is-sms-pumping-fraud.md): SMS pumping fraud happens when fraudsters take advantage of a phone number input field to receive a one-time passcode, an app download link, or anything else via SMS. The messages are sent to a range of numbers controlled by a specific mobile network operator (MNO) and the fraudsters get a share of the generated revenue. 
* [What is the Telephone Consumer Protection Act (TCPA)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-telephone-consumer-protection-act-tcpa.md): The TCPA (Telephone Consumer Protection Act) is a federal statute enacted in 1991 designed to safeguard consumer privacy. This legislation restricts telemarketing communications via voice calls, SMS texts, and fax.
* [What is Toll Fraud?](/docs/glossary/what-is-toll-fraud.md): Toll fraud, also known as International Revenue Sharing Fraud (IRSF), happens when an application is exploited to generate a high volume of voice calls to the fraudster's own international premium rate numbers. The victim of the toll fraud bears the entire financial responsibility for each minute of the call.
* [What is the Twilio Markup Language (TwiML)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-twilio-markup-language-twiml.md): TwiML, the Twilio Markup Language, is an XML-based language that instructs Twilio on how to handle events such as incoming and outgoing calls, SMS messages, and MMS messages. Learn how TwiML verbs and nouns work.
* [What is Two Factor Authentication?](/docs/glossary/what-is-two-factor-authentication-2fa.md): Two-factor authentication (2FA) adds an extra layer of security to your user's account login by requiring two types of authentication: usually something your user knows and something they have.
* [What is UCS-2 Character Encoding?](/docs/glossary/what-is-ucs-2-character-encoding.md): UCS-2 is a character encoding standard that represents characters with a fixed-length 16 bits (2 bytes). GSM networks use it as a fallback when a message cannot be encoded using GSM-7 or needs more than 128 characters.
* [What is Unicode?](/docs/glossary/what-is-unicode.md): Unicode is an international character encoding standard that provides a unique number for every character across languages and scripts, making almost all characters accessible across platforms, programs, and devices.
* [What is a Voice API?](/docs/glossary/what-is-voice-api.md): A voice API lets software developers make and receive phone calls programmatically, bridging the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN) and internet applications without specialized telecommunications knowledge or hardware.
* [What is VoIP (Voice Over IP)?](/docs/glossary/what-is-voip.md): Voice Over Internet Protocol (VoIP) is a category of hardware and software that enables voice calls to be made and received over the internet.
* [What is a Long Code?](/docs/glossary/what-long-code-phone-number.md): A long code number is a standard phone number used to send and receive voice calls and SMS messages. Phone numbers are typically called "long codes" (10-digit numbers in many countries) when comparing them with SMS short codes (5-6 digit numbers).
* [How long can a message be?](/docs/glossary/what-sms-character-limit.md): The number of characters that a service can transmit varies according to the service protocol and character encoding.
* [What is an SMS tracker?](/docs/glossary/what-sms-tracker.md): An SMS tracker is software that uncovers detailed information about the delivery and content of text and picture messages. It allows anyone to analyze an individual message or group of messages to see delivery patterns, encoding details, and error conditions.
   
* [What is UTF-8?](/docs/glossary/what-utf-8.md): UTF-8 is a variable-width character encoding standard that uses between one and four eight-bit bytes to represent all valid Unicode code points.
* [What is a Virtual Phone Number?](/docs/glossary/what-virtual-phone-number.md): A virtual phone number is a standard telephone number that is not locked down to a specific phone. A virtual number can route a voice call or text message to any phone or workflow. With virtual numbers powered by an API, complex software workflows can be built that are triggered by calls and texts.
* [What is Voice Trace?](/docs/glossary/what-voice-trace.md): Voice trace is a voice quality troubleshooting feature that captures the media stream for calls and stores them for Twilio Support to use in their investigations. 

## Identity and Access Management (IAM)

* [Access Control](/docs/iam/access-control/overview.md): Use Twilio role-based access control (RBAC) to grant granular access to resources. Manage role assignments and apply least-privilege access for users.
* [RBAC in Legacy Console](/docs/iam/access-control/rbac-legacy-console.md): Learn how to manage role assignments in the Legacy Console.
* [RBAC in Twilio Console](/docs/iam/access-control/rbac-twilio-console.md): This document describes how users can manage Role Assignments in Twilio Console
* [Role Assignment API reference](/docs/iam/access-control/role-assignment-resource.md): Complete API reference for the Twilio Role Assignment API. Learn how to list, create, and delete role assignments using OAuth 2.0.
* [Role API reference](/docs/iam/access-control/role-resource.md): Complete API reference for the Twilio Role API. Learn how to list available roles for your organization using OAuth 2.0.
* [Types of Roles](/docs/iam/access-control/types-of-roles.md): Learn about the four types of Twilio RBAC roles: general roles, built-in roles, organization roles, and resource-based roles for user assignment.
* [Access Tokens](/docs/iam/access-tokens.md): Create short-lived Access Tokens to authenticate Twilio client-side SDKs like Voice, Conversations, and Video.
* [API keys overview](/docs/iam/api-keys.md): Authenticate your requests to Twilio's APIs with API keys. Keep your Twilio Account credentials safe and take control of the lifecycle of your API keys.
* [REST API: Key Resource v1](/docs/iam/api-keys/key-resource-v1.md): Full API reference for the Key resource in the Twilio API. Learn how to create, fetch, list, update, and delete API keys.
* [REST API: Key Resource v2010](/docs/iam/api-keys/key-resource-v2010.md): Full API reference for the API Key resource in the Twilio API. Learn how to create, fetch (get), read (list), and update API keys to authenticate to the REST API and create and revoke access tokens.
* [Create API keys in Twilio Console](/docs/iam/api-keys/keys-in-console.md): Authenticate your requests to Twilio's APIs with API keys. Create and manage API keys in Twilio Console.
* [Restricted API keys](/docs/iam/api-keys/restricted-api-keys.md): Leverage role-based access control (RBAC) with Twilio's Restricted API keys. Grant granular API permissions for Twilio APIs.
* [REST API: Accounts](/docs/iam/api/account.md): Full API reference for the Account resource in the Twilio API. Learn how to create, fetch (get), read (list), and update Accounts and Subaccounts.
