## Studio

* [Studio](/docs/studio.md): Use Twilio Studio's low-code builder to create and manage communication workflows like SMS chatbots and order notifications.
* [New Studio engine](/docs/studio/new-studio-engine.md): Learn about the new Studio engine, how Twilio migrates existing Studio flows to it, and how to check which engine processed an execution.
* [Execution Resource](/docs/studio/rest-api/execution.md): API reference for the Execution resource in the Studio REST API. Sample code shows how to create, fetch (get), read (list), update, and delete executions.
* [Flow](/docs/studio/rest-api/flow.md): Full API reference for the Flow resource in the Studio API with code samples showing how to fetch (get), read (list), and delete a Studio Flow.
* [REST API v1](/docs/studio/rest-api.md): Overview of the REST API v1 for Studio including basic usage, authentication requirements, and SDKs.
* [Execution Context](/docs/studio/rest-api/v2/execution-context.md): API reference for the Execution Context resource of a Studio Flow. Learn how to fetch an Execution Context using the REST API for Studio.
* [Execution](/docs/studio/rest-api/v2/execution.md): API reference for the Execution resource of a Studio Flow. Learn how to create and modify an Execution using the REST API for Studio.
* [Flow](/docs/studio/rest-api/v2/flow.md): API reference for the Flow resource of a Studio Flow. Learn how to create and modify a Flow using the REST API for Studio.
* [REST API v2](/docs/studio/rest-api/v2.md): Learn the basics of triggering Studio flows programmatically with the Studio REST API.
* [Step](/docs/studio/rest-api/v2/step.md): API reference for the Step resource of a Studio Flow Execution. Learn how to fetch and list Steps of an Execution using the REST API for Studio.
* [Studio Flows API Quickstart](/docs/studio/rest-api/v2/studio-flows-api-quickstart.md): Get started with the Twilio Studio Flows REST API. Learn how to create a Flow in Console, then fetch and update its JSON definition with cURL and the API.
* [Test User](/docs/studio/rest-api/v2/test-user.md): API reference for fetching Test Users of a Studio Flow.
* [Subflows](/docs/studio/subflows.md): Overview of Subflows in Studio including basic usage, working with variables, limitations, and testing.
* [Customer Support Menu with WhatsApp and Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/customer-support-menu.md): Build a simple customer support bot in WhatsApp using Twilio Studio. Answer common questions and take the first step to build out your WhatsApp presence!
* [Build a Chatbot with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/how-to-build-a-chatbot.md): Learn how to build a Chatbot with Studio, Twilio's drag-and-drop virtual workflow builder.
* [How to build an IVR with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/how-to-build-an-ivr.md): Create IVR systems without coding using Twilio Studio's drag-and-drop editor. Build and route calls efficiently with speech or keypress inputs.
* [Conduct an SMS Survey with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/how-to-conduct-a-survey.md): Build a Twilio Studio Flow that sends an SMS survey, validates replies with the Split Based On widget, and posts responses to your server via REST API.
* [Forward Calls with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/how-to-forward-calls.md): Set up call forwarding with just one Twilio Studio widget. Divert calls to your personal or business number and never miss a critical call again.
* [Post Messages to Slack with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/how-to-post-sms-to-slack.md): Post inbound SMS to a Slack feed with Twilio Studio and an incoming Slack webhook.
* [Send Appointment Reminders with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/how-to-send-appointment-reminders.md): Build a Twilio Studio Flow that sends SMS appointment reminders via REST API, collects confirm or cancel replies, and posts the response to your server.
* [Set up an SMS Autoresponder with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/how-to-set-up-auto-responder.md): Use Twilio Studio to create an autoresponder to greet incoming SMS or Whatsapp messages with a personalized response.
* [Tutorials](/docs/studio/tutorials.md): List of step-by-step tutorials demonstrating how to use Studio.
* [Use RCS messaging with Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/use-rcs-with-studio.md): Learn how to enable RCS messaging in your Twilio Studio Flow to send and receive RCS messages.
* [Using Twilio Conversations with Studio](/docs/studio/tutorials/using-conversations-with-studio.md): Guide on how to use the Twilio Conversations integration with Studio.
* [Get Started with Twilio Studio](/docs/studio/user-guide/get-started.md): Overview of setting up a Studio application including working with widgets, Liquid variables, and troubleshooting flows.
* [Studio User Guide](/docs/studio/user-guide.md): Design, deploy and scale customer communications seamlessly with Twilio Studio. Designed for cross-functional teams, anyone can create and modify flows.
* [Liquid Template Language](/docs/studio/user-guide/liquid-template-language.md): Learn to use the Liquid Template Language to render dynamic content in Twilio Studio.
* [Studio FAQ](/docs/studio/user-guide/studio-faq.md): Frequently asked questions about Twilio Studio.
* [Studio Troubleshooting](/docs/studio/user-guide/studio-troubleshooting.md): Guide demonstrating how to use logs and revisions effectively when attempting to find problems occurring within a Flow.
* [Call Recording widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/call-recording.md): Learn how to use the Call Recording widget in your Twilio Studio flow to switch Voice Call Recording ON or OFF at any point during an incoming call flow.
* [Connect Call To widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/connect-call.md): Learn how to connect an in-progress call with another phone number, Client user, SIM, SIP endpoint, or conference with the Connect Call To widget.
* [Connect Virtual Agent widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/connect-virtual-agent.md): Learn how to connect a Twilio Voice call or Twilio Conversations to a Dialogflow CX agent using the Connect Virtual Agent widget.
* [Enqueue Call widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/enqueue-call.md): Enqueue a current call managed with Studio into a call queue with the Enqueue Call widget and play music for your caller until they are dequeued.
* [Gather Input On Call widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/gather-input-call.md): Gather a user's input while they are on a call with the Gather Input On Call widget for Studio and collect DTMF keypresses or text from speech recognition.
* [Make HTTP Request widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/http-request.md): Learn how to interact with code that lives outside of Studio with the Make HTTP Request widget to communicate between Studio and business logic.
* [Widget Library](/docs/studio/widget-library.md): Widgets are individual items that can be dragged onto the Flow canvas in Twilio Studio. They represent pieces of logic, and can connect to each other via Transitions.
* [Make Outgoing Call widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/make-outgoing-call.md): Learn how to dial a contact's phone number from within your Studio Flow with the Make Call widget.
* [Record Voicemail widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/record-voicemail.md): Record and transcribe a caller's message with the Record Voicemail widget.
* [Run Function widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/run-function.md): Execute Twilio Functions from within your Studio flow with the Run Functions widget and communicate between your flow and more complex business logic.
* [Say/Play widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/sayplay.md): Play a recorded message or dictate text to a user on call with the Say/Play widget in your Studio flow.
* [Send to Flex widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/send-flex.md): Learn how to transfer an incoming message or call to Flex with the Send to Flex widget.
* [Send Message widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/send-message.md): Send a message to a user with the Send Message widget and respond to users that message your application from an SMS or Programmable Chat channel.
* [Send and Wait For Reply widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/send-wait-reply.md): Learn how to send an outgoing message, wait for a reply, and collect the user's response with the Send and Wait For Reply widget.
* [Set Variables widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/set-variables.md): Learn how to save key/value pairs in the global context of your Studio flow with the SetVariables widget and dynamically update data as your flow executes.
* [Split Based On... widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/split-based-on.md): Learn how to split your Studio flow and connect to specific widgets based on user conditions with the Split Based On widget.
* [Trigger (Start) widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/trigger-start.md): Begin the Studio Flow with the Trigger (Start) widget and communicate between Studio and the parts of your business logic.
* [TwiML Redirect widget](/docs/studio/widget-library/twiml-redirect.md): Redirect a Voice Call to be handled outside of Studio with the TwiML Redirect widget and pass parameters to your TwiML Redirect URL.

## Sync: Shared state in the cloud

* [Sync: Shared state in the cloud](/docs/sync.md): Sync, Twilio's state synchronization service, offers two-way real-time communication between browsers, mobiles, and the cloud. Find REST API docs, tutorials, user guides, and quickstarts to get you syncing in your web and mobile apps, fast.

## TaskRouter: Skills-based routing for contact centers

* [Activity Based Routing](/docs/taskrouter/activity-based-routing.md): Use TaskRouter activity-based routing to target or exclude Workers based on their Activity state, so you can route high-value Tasks and control Worker eligibility.
* [Activity Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/activity.md): Learn about the Activity resource, which describes the current state of your Workers, including Activity properties, and how to create, read, update, and delete Activities.
* [Event Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/event.md): TaskRouter logs Events for each state change in the Workspace, allowing you to build historical reporting and auditing for your routing use case.
* [TaskRouter Events reference](/docs/taskrouter/api/event/reference.md): Learn about TaskRouter Events and find descriptions of all Event Callbacks.
* [TaskRouter: REST API Reference](/docs/taskrouter/api.md): An overview of the TaskRouter API including the base URL for all TaskRouter resources and links to guides for best practices when building with this API.
* [Task Reservation Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/reservations.md): Full API reference for the Task Reservations resource in the TaskRouter API. Find sample code that shows how to fetch (get) and update reservations.
* [Task Channel resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/task-channel.md): A full API reference for the Task Channel resource, including code samples to create, fetch, read, update, and delete Task Channels.
* [Task Queue resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/task-queue.md): Full API reference for the TaskQueue resource in the TaskRouter API. Find sample code showing how to create, fetch (get), read (list), update, and delete.
* [Task Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/task.md): A TaskRouter Task represents a single item of work waiting to be processed. This overview covers task attributes, versions, and a Task lifecycle.
* [REST API: TaskQueue Statistics](/docs/taskrouter/api/taskqueue-statistics.md): Full API reference for the TaskQueue Statistics resource in the TaskRouter API. Sample code shows how to retrieve real-time, cumulative, and instance TaskQueue Statistics.
* [Worker Channel Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker-channel.md): Full API reference for the Worker Channels resource in the TaskRouter API. Sample code shows how to fetch (get), read (list), and update worker channels.
* [Worker Reservation resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker-reservation.md): Full API reference for the TaskRouter API's Worker Reservation resource. Sample code shows how to fetch (get), read (list), and update a WorkerReservation.
* [Worker Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker.md): Twilio TaskRouter allows you to create, update, delete, and retrieve Workers, which are entities that can perform tasks. A Worker might be an agent working in a call center, or a salesperson handling leads.
* [Worker Statistics Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker/statistics.md): Full API reference for the TaskRouter Worker Statistics resource. Code samples show how to fetch a resource and retrieve worker statistics.
* [Workflow Statistics Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/workflow-statistics.md): Learn about the Workflow Statistics resource, how to retrieve Workflow Statistics, RealTime Statistics, and Cumulative Statistics.
* [Workflow Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/workflow.md): Full API reference for the Workflow resource in the Taskrouter REST API. Sample code shows how to create, fetch, read, update, and delete a Workflow.
* [REST API: Workspace Statistics](/docs/taskrouter/api/workspace-statistics.md): Learn about the Workspace Statistics TaskRouter provides. Find sample code for how to retrieve statistics, real-time statistics, and cumulative statistics for Workspaces.
* [Workspace Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/workspace.md): A full reference for the Workspace Resource of the TaskRouter API, with code samples to create, read, update, fetch, update, and delete a Workspace.
* [Call Control Concepts | Appendix | Web Sequence Diagrams](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/call-control-concepts-web-sequence-diagrams.md): Web sequence diagrams for TaskRouter call control concepts, including blind (cold) transfer and warm transfer flows between caller, Twilio, and your app.
* [Call Control Concepts](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/call-control-concepts.md): A conceptual overview of best practices for TaskRouter call control for reporting and dashboard accuracy, including holds, transfers, monitor, and barge.
* [Using Chat and TaskRouter together | Appendix | Web Sequence Diagrams](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/chat-taskrouter-web-sequence-diagrams.md): Web sequence diagram for using Programmable Chat with TaskRouter, showing the flow from a customer starting a chat to an agent handling and completing the task.
* [Using Chat and TaskRouter Together](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/chat-taskrouter.md): Learn best practices for using Programmable Chat with TaskRouter for Contact Center use cases.
* [Dashboards](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/dashboards.md): Learn about common requirements for real-time dashboards for your TaskRouter contact center including data flow, summaries, queues, and agents.
* [Using Email and TaskRouter Together](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/email-taskrouter.md): Learn how to add Email as a channel to your contact center, by combining a REST based third party email service with TaskRouter.
* [Contact Center Blueprint](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint.md): Learn how to build a contact center on Twilio. Reference code samples, architecture guidance, and design philosophies as you build out your contact center.
* [Reporting](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/reporting.md): Common reporting requirements for a contact center, including real-time dashboards and historical reports to ensure you have all the data you need.
* [Using SMS and TaskRouter Together | Web Sequence Diagrams](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/sms-taskrouter-web-sequence-diagrams.md): Web sequence diagram source for using SMS and TaskRouter together in a contact center. Copy the diagram code into websequencediagrams.com to customize the flow.
* [Using SMS and TaskRouter Together](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/sms-taskrouter.md): Learn the best practice for adding SMS as a channel to your contact center by combining Twilio Numbers, Programmable SMS, Programmable Chat, and TaskRouter.
* [Using TaskRouter Expressions](/docs/taskrouter/expression-syntax.md): TaskRouter uses a SQL-like expression syntax for binding Workers to TaskQueues and filtering Tasks into TaskQueues in a Workflow configuration. Learn how to compose expressions from  constants, JSON keys, as well as logical and comparison operators.
* [Assigning Tasks to Workers: Handling Assignment Callbacks](/docs/taskrouter/handle-assignment-callbacks.md): Learn how to handle Assignment Callbacks in TaskRouter, including parameters included in the HTTP POST request and ways to respond in your code.
* [How TaskRouter Works](/docs/taskrouter/how-taskrouter-works.md): Learn the basics of how TaskRouter, our system for distributing tasks in your contact center, works. Includes an example application flow and useful links.
* [TaskRouter: Skills-based routing for contact centers](/docs/taskrouter.md): TaskRouter is a skills-based routing system that provides the heart of a contact center that you can control from your code.
* [TaskRouter.js v1: Integrating TaskRouter to your browser-based applications](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1.md): Learn about integrating TaskRouter into your browser-based applications using the Javascript SDK.
* [TaskRouter.js v1: Constructing JWTs](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace/constructing-jwts.md): Generate JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) from your server to authorize which TaskRouter resources a client-side TaskRouter.js v1 application can access.
* [TaskRouter.js v1 Workspace: Managing resources in the browser](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace.md): The TaskRouter Javascript SDK allows developers to interact with the entire TaskRouter REST API by a simple JS API.
* [TaskRouter.js v1 TaskQueue: Managing a TaskQueue resource in the browser](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace/taskqueue.md): Learn how to manage a TaskQueue resource in the browser using the TaskRouter Javascript SDK.
* [TaskRouter.js v1 Worker: Managing workers in the browser](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace/worker.md): Comprehensive documentation about TaskRouter's JavaScript workers, used to route tasks in your web-based applications.
* [TaskRouter.js v2: Reconnect Logic](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v2/reconnect.md): Documentation about how TaskRouter SDK v2 is reconnecting the WebSocket after disconnection.
* [TaskRouter.js v2 and v3: Integrating TaskRouter with your browser-based applications](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk.md): Learn about integrating TaskRouter into your browser-based applications using TaskRouter JavaScript SDK v2 and v3.
* [Lifecycle of a Task: Task state](/docs/taskrouter/lifecycle-task-state.md): Learn the basics of the various states of a Task, the core of TaskRouter, including the states a Task can go through as it moves from pending to completed.
* [Lifecycle of a Task: Workflows and Assignment](/docs/taskrouter/lifecycle-task-workflows-and-assignment.md): Learn about the lifecycle of a TaskRouter Task, how it moves through a Workflow, and how it gets assigned to a Worker.
* [TaskRouter resource limits](/docs/taskrouter/limits.md): Twilio TaskRouter has default limits on certain resources, configuration parameters, and endpoints. If you are running into these limits, please reach out to our support team. We're happy to help work with you to increase some of these limits or help optimize how you use TaskRouter.
* [Upgrade to TaskRouter SDK version 2](/docs/taskrouter/migrate-js-sdk-to-v2.md): Migrate from TaskRouter JavaScript SDK version 1 to version 2: callbacks to promises, worker activity changes, event listening, and breaking changes.
* [Multitasking](/docs/taskrouter/multitasking.md): TaskRouter Multitasking allows a worker to handle multiple tasks of different types in parallel.
* [Ordering Workers](/docs/taskrouter/order-workers.md): Learn how to use the order\_by expression to designate in what order Workers are assigned tasks when using TaskRouter.
* [Queue Ordering](/docs/taskrouter/queue-ordering-last-first-out-lifo.md): Learn about how TaskQueue handles ordering with First In, First Out (FIFO) or Last In, First Out (LIFO).
* [TaskRouter .Net Quickstart](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp.md): In this Quickstart, build a simplified version of a multi-channel sales and technical support system using Twilio TaskRouter and .Net.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using Assignment Callback Instructions](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-accept-callback.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation automatically with an assignment callback instruction, by returning an accept instruction in a synchronous HTTP response from your C# server.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-accept-rest.md): Accept or reject a TaskRouter Task Reservation by making an HTTP POST request to a Reservation instance resource with the TaskSid and ReservationSid in C#.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Set up the Assignment Callback URL](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-assignment-callback.md): Set up a TaskRouter Assignment Callback URL with a C# web server so TaskRouter can notify your application when a Worker is reserved to perform a Task.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Create a Task using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-create-task-rest.md): Create a TaskRouter Task with the REST API in C#, then bring an eligible Worker online to trigger an automatic Task Reservation and assignment callback.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure TaskQueues](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-add-task-queues.md): Add and configure TaskRouter TaskQueues that route Tasks to Workers by language skill, using SQL-like Target Workers expressions in the TaskRouter console.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure Workers](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-add-workers.md): Add and configure TaskRouter Workers in the console, defining their skills with JSON attributes so TaskRouter can route Tasks based on language and other criteria.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure a Workflow](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-add-workflow.md): Add and configure a TaskRouter Workflow with routing configurations that direct incoming Tasks to the correct TaskQueue based on the caller's selected language.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Create a Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-create-workspace.md): Create a TaskRouter Workspace, the container element for any TaskRouter application, using the TaskRouter console or the REST API.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Create a TaskRouter Task using \<Enqueue>](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-create-task.md): Create a TaskRouter Task from an incoming phone call in C# using the TwiML \<Enqueue> verb, routing the call to a Task Queue by selected language.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Dequeue a Call to a Worker](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-dequeue-call.md): Dequeue a TaskRouter call to an eligible Worker in C#. Return a dequeue Assignment Instruction to bridge the caller to the Worker's contact\_uri.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: End-to-End Phone Call Task Assignment](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-end-to-end.md): Test the end-to-end TaskRouter phone call flow in C#: create a Task from an incoming call, route it to an available Worker, and bridge the call.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Receive an Incoming Call](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-receive-call.md): Receive an incoming Twilio phone call in C# and prompt the caller for a language with TwiML \<Gather> before creating a TaskRouter Task.
* [Control Worker Activities using Worker.js: Add an Agent UI to our Project](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/agent-ui-add-project.md): Add a real-time agent user interface to a TaskRouter Java app using TaskRouter.js, letting Workers view and control their own Activity state.
* [TaskRouter Java Quickstart](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java.md): In this Quickstart, build a simplified version of a multichannel sales and technical support system using Twilio TaskRouter and Java.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using Assignment Callback Instructions](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-accept-callback.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation automatically by returning an 'accept' assignment instruction from your Assignment Callback URL, using Java and the REST API.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-accept-rest.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation with the REST API by making a POST request to the Reservation instance resource with ReservationStatus set to accepted.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Set up the Assignment Callback URL](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-assignment-callback.md): Set up a TaskRouter Assignment Callback URL with a Java servlet so TaskRouter can notify your server when a Worker is reserved to perform a Task.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Create a Task using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-create-task-rest.md): Create a TaskRouter Task with the REST API using Java or curl, then bring an eligible Worker online to trigger an automatic Task Reservation.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure TaskQueues](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-add-task-queues.md): Add and configure TaskRouter TaskQueues that route Tasks to Workers by language skill, using Target Workers Expressions with the HAS operator.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure Workers](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-add-workers.md): Add and configure TaskRouter Workers in the console, defining JSON Worker Attributes such as language skills to route incoming Tasks by capability.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure a Workflow](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-add-workflow.md): Add and configure a TaskRouter Workflow with routing configurations that match incoming Tasks to the right Task Queue based on the caller selected language.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Create a Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-create-workspace.md): Create a TaskRouter Workspace, the container element for any TaskRouter application, using the REST API or the TaskRouter console in the Twilio Console.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Create a TaskRouter Task using \<Enqueue>](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/twiml-create-task.md): Create a TaskRouter Task from an incoming Twilio phone call using the TwiML \<Enqueue> verb in Java, routing the caller to a Task Queue based on their selected language.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Dequeue a Call to a Worker](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/twiml-dequeue-call.md): Use a TaskRouter dequeue Assignment Instruction in Java to connect an enqueued caller to an eligible Worker at their contact\_uri when the Worker becomes available.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: End-to-End Phone Call Task Assignment](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/twiml-end-to-end.md): Test the end-to-end TaskRouter phone call flow in Java: create a Task from an incoming call, assign it to an available Worker, and bridge the dequeued call.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Receive an Incoming Call](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/twiml-receive-call.md): Handle an incoming Twilio phone call in Java with TaskRouter TwiML, using \<Gather> and \<Say> to prompt the caller to select a preferred language before creating a Task.
* [Control Worker Activities using Worker.js: Add an Agent UI to our Project](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/agent-ui-add-project.md): Build a real-time agent UI for Twilio TaskRouter using TaskRouter.js and PHP, letting Workers change Activity state and receive Reservation event notifications.
* [TaskRouter: PHP quickstart](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php.md): In this Quickstart, build a simplified version of a multichannel sales and technical support system using Twilio TaskRouter and PHP.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using Assignment Callback Instructions](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/reservations-accept-callback.md): Accept a Twilio TaskRouter Reservation automatically by returning an 'accept' assignment instruction from your Assignment Callback URL in a synchronous HTTP response.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/reservations-accept-rest.md): Accept or reject a Twilio TaskRouter Reservation by sending an HTTP POST request to the Reservation instance resource with the TaskSid and ReservationSid.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Set up the Assignment Callback URL](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/reservations-assignment-callback.md): Set up an Assignment Callback URL for Twilio TaskRouter so the system can notify your PHP server when a Worker is reserved to perform a Task.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Create a Task using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/reservations-create-task-rest.md): Learn how to create a Task using the REST API.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure TaskQueues](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/setup-add-task-queues.md): Add and configure Twilio TaskRouter TaskQueues that assign Tasks to Workers based on Worker attributes, using SQL-like Target Workers Expressions for language routing.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure Workers](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/setup-add-workers.md): Learn how to add and configure Workers in this part of the Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace guide.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure a Workflow](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/setup-add-workflow.md): Add and configure a TaskRouter Workflow with Routing Configurations that route incoming Tasks to the right Task Queue based on the caller-selected language.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Create a Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/setup-create-workspace.md): Create a TaskRouter Workspace, the container element for any TaskRouter application, using the TaskRouter console or the REST API.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Create a TaskRouter Task using \<Enqueue>](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/twiml-create-task.md): Create a TaskRouter Task from an incoming phone call using the TwiML \<Enqueue> verb, routing the caller to a Task Queue based on their selected language.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Dequeue a Call to a Worker](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/twiml-dequeue-call.md): Use a TaskRouter dequeue assignment instruction to bridge a queued caller to an eligible Worker at their contact\_uri when the Worker becomes available.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: End-to-End Phone Call Task Assignment](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/twiml-end-to-end.md): Test the end-to-end TaskRouter flow: create a Task from an incoming phone call, make a Worker available, and bridge the call to the eligible Worker.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Receive an Incoming Call](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/php/twiml-receive-call.md): Receive an incoming Twilio phone call with TwiML and prompt the caller to select a language using the \<Gather> verb before creating a TaskRouter Task.
* [Control Worker Activities using Worker.js: Add an Agent UI to our Project](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/agent-ui-add-project.md): Build an agent UI for your TaskRouter Python project with Flask and TaskRouter.js, generating a Worker capability token and rendering an agent.html template.
* [TaskRouter Python Quickstart](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python.md): In this Quickstart, build a simplified version of a multi-channel sales and technical support system using Twilio TaskRouter and Python.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using Assignment Callback Instructions](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/reservations-accept-callback.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation automatically by returning an 'accept' assignment instruction from your Python Flask Assignment Callback URL over a synchronous HTTP response.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/reservations-accept-rest.md): Accept or reject a TaskRouter Task Reservation by sending an HTTP POST to the Reservation instance resource with ReservationStatus, using Python or curl.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Set up the Assignment Callback URL](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/reservations-assignment-callback.md): Set up a TaskRouter Assignment Callback URL with a Python Flask server and expose it to Twilio using ngrok so TaskRouter can notify you when a Worker is reserved.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Create a Task using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/reservations-create-task-rest.md): Create a TaskRouter Task with the REST API using Python and curl, then bring an eligible Worker online to trigger a Reservation at your Assignment Callback URL.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure TaskQueues](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/setup-add-task-queues.md): Create and configure TaskRouter TaskQueues that route Tasks to Workers by attribute, using target Worker expressions to match language skills.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and configure Workers](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/setup-add-workers.md): Add and configure TaskRouter Workers, the people or processes that perform Tasks, and set JSON attributes such as language skills for routing.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure a Workflow](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/setup-add-workflow.md): Add and configure a TaskRouter Workflow with Routing Configurations that match Task attributes to the correct TaskQueue for language-based routing.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Create a Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/setup-create-workspace.md): Create a TaskRouter Workspace, the container element for a TaskRouter application, using the TaskRouter console or the REST API.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Create a TaskRouter Task using \<Enqueue>](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/twiml-create-task.md): Create a TaskRouter Task from an incoming phone call in Python using the TwiML \<Enqueue> verb to route callers to the correct TaskQueue.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Dequeue a Call to a Worker](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/twiml-dequeue-call.md): Use a TaskRouter dequeue Assignment Instruction in Python to call an eligible Worker at their contact\_uri and bridge them to the waiting caller.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: End-to-End Phone Call Task Assignment](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/twiml-end-to-end.md): Test the TaskRouter Python TwiML call flow end-to-end: create a Task from an incoming call, assign it to a Worker, and bridge the two parties.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Receive an Incoming Call](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/python/twiml-receive-call.md): Configure a Twilio phone number and Python Flask endpoint to receive an incoming call and create a TaskRouter Task using TwiML in the Python Quickstart.
* [Control Worker Activities using Worker.js: Add an Agent UI to our Project](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/agent-ui-add-project.md): Build a realtime agent UI with TaskRouter.js and a Ruby Sinatra endpoint that lets a Worker control their Activity state and see incoming call events.
* [TaskRouter Ruby Quickstart](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby.md): Build a multi-channel sales and support routing app with Twilio TaskRouter and Ruby. This Quickstart covers Workspaces, Tasks, Reservations, TwiML, and TaskRouter.js.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using Assignment Callback Instructions](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/reservations-accept-callback.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation automatically by returning an accept assignment instruction in a synchronous HTTP response from your Assignment Callback URL in Ruby.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/reservations-accept-rest.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation with the REST API by sending an HTTP POST to a Reservation instance resource with ReservationStatus set to accepted, using Ruby or curl.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Set up the Assignment Callback URL](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/reservations-assignment-callback.md): Set up an Assignment Callback URL with a Ruby Sinatra server so TaskRouter can notify you when a Worker is reserved for a Task, exposed to Twilio with ngrok.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Create a Task using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/reservations-create-task-rest.md): Create a TaskRouter Task with the REST API in Ruby, bring an eligible Worker online, and observe the Task Reservation lifecycle in this Quickstart step.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure TaskQueues](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/setup-add-task-queues.md): Add and configure TaskRouter TaskQueues that route Tasks to Workers by language skill using Target Workers Expressions in this TaskRouter Quickstart step.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure Workers](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/setup-add-workers.md): Create and configure TaskRouter Workers with JSON language attributes so TaskRouter can route calls to agents by skill in this TaskRouter Quickstart step.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure a Workflow](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/setup-add-workflow.md): Add a TaskRouter Workflow with Routing Configurations that direct Tasks to the right TaskQueue based on the caller selected language in this Quickstart step.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Create a Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/setup-create-workspace.md): Create a TaskRouter Workspace, the container element for any TaskRouter application, using the TaskRouter console in this TaskRouter Quickstart step.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Understanding Activities](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/setup-understanding-activities.md): Understand TaskRouter Activities, the discrete states a Worker can be in, and how the Available property determines eligibility for new Task Reservations.
* [Part 1. Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/setup.md): Set up a TaskRouter Workspace in Ruby by creating Workers, TaskQueues, a Workflow, and Activities in Part 1 of the TaskRouter Ruby Quickstart.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Create a TaskRouter Task using \<Enqueue>](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/twiml-create-task.md): Create a TaskRouter Task from an incoming phone call using the TwiML \<Enqueue> verb in Ruby, routing callers to a TaskQueue by their selected language.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Dequeue a Call to a Worker](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/ruby/twiml-dequeue-call.md): Use a TaskRouter dequeue Assignment Instruction in Ruby to bridge a queued caller to an eligible Worker at their contact\_uri when the Worker becomes available.
